-- title:
--   Smooth proper model over an infinite perfect field
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an infinite perfect field and let $L$ be a field equipped with a $K$-algebra structure, $K$ and $L$ allowed to lie in different universes. Assume there is some $x \in L$ which is transcendental over $K$ and such that $L$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$. Then the type `CurveModel K L` is nonempty: there exist a scheme $C$ in the universe of $K$ and a morphism $C \to \operatorname{Spec} K$ such that $C$ is integral, the morphism is proper and smooth of relative dimension $1$, together with a ring isomorphism $\mathrm{ffEquiv} : L \to C.\mathrm{functionField}$ (the stalk of the structure sheaf at the generic point) whose restriction to $K$ agrees with the map $K \to C.\mathrm{functionField}$ induced by the structure morphism, and a bijection from the closed points of $C$ onto the places of $L/K$, where a place is by definition a valuation subring of $L$ containing the image of $K$, different from $L$ itself, and a principal ideal ring; the bijection satisfies, for each closed point $x$, that the image in $L$ under $\mathrm{ffEquiv}^{-1}$ of the local ring $\mathcal{O}_{C,x}$, viewed inside $C.\mathrm{functionField}$, is exactly the valuation subring of the corresponding place. Finally, every finite subset of $C$ is contained in an affine open subscheme.
--
--   This is the characteristic-free existence of the smooth proper model of a one-variable function field over an infinite perfect base field, in the packaged form used throughout the treatment of curves and their Jacobians; it is the input to the constructions of divisor class groups and of degree-zero Picard groups, for instance in the results producing Jacobian data over algebraically closed fields and in the analysis of the Frobenius pushforward on $\mathrm{Pic}^0$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_CurveModel_nonempty_of_perfectField.lean

